A||||7||artherd||Have to remove all AC parts  to put into a non-ac car, in ONE DAY!||||3||
Z||000000||artherd||10-01-2001||12:24 AM||||Ok, just wanting to be sure I don't miss anything.<P>I've got to pull the *entire* AC system out of a so-equiped Fiero, for transplant into a non-ac Fiero.<P>Any gotchas? (i've already got everything behind the seats, ie compressor.)<P>I have pulled the cold side core, as well as the resivor (big Al cylinder)  and pannel with the fan, etc on it.<P>Main thing is, I am having a devil of a time removing the larger box that holds the heater core and presumabely other ducting to the inside of the car.  I have all the bolts I can get at from outside the car off, and it still will not budge!<P>Also, that so called electrical 'connector' is stuck like it was welded, I'm just going to try to unbolt it....<P>Do I need to remove the dash (uugh) to get this stuff out?<P>What is different in the AC cars that I need to salvage?<P>I have to do this much faster than I'd like to, hence the post ;)<P>I don't want to be saying "oh crap, forgot that!"<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000001||David Gamache||10-01-2001||01:05 AM||||Your in for a job.  The dash and everything behind it will need to be removed and the "large black box" housing the ducts and ac/heater controls will just barely have enough room to pull out and the replacement unit is just about as much fun to re-install.  Remeber to get all the mounting hardware in front of the radiator where the a/c parts are and the fan motor assembly. The wiring harness and relays are different so you will need the complete wire harness for the engine, front compartment and interior.  The ecm is the same so you will not need to replace yours.  The hoses and brackets under the car are obviously needed along with new vent/a/c/heater controls.  I have done this conversion on an 87 coupe.  it was a bit of work but worked great once it was done.  You may want to install new pressure switches on your compressor if you are using a used comperssor since the high and low pressure switches often fail before the compressor and you have to recharge the a/c system after having the system work for a short while.  Also consider the 134a conversion. It is an inexpensive alternative to the r12 and works well especially when the 134a cooling booster is added.  All that is needed is a couple of 134a adapter fittings, a new accumulator, in line filter, and complete system flush, which is recommedded anyway.  The new freeze 12 ia another alternative that is compatable with r12 as a replacement charge, but it is a chemical compound and the compounds may seperate over time and fail is what I have been told so far.    ||||reg||1
Z||000002||GarethWright||10-01-2001||01:10 AM||||You may also need a new alternator, as when I replaced my alternator I had to specify whether I had AC or not on my car||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||10-01-2001||02:25 AM||||Without being a nay-sayer, give it two unless you are talking dusk 'till dawn. There is a fair amount of electrical work too.||||reg||1
Z||000004||artherd||10-01-2001||06:29 AM||||Thanks guys, I'm actually going to have about another two days it seems  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I should have mentioned I'm going to be doing a bit of 'modifications' to the system as it goes into the new car (going to build something like the 'set a temp and forget' as found orgionally in Merceded cars, and now in may modern vehicles)<P>So fortunately I won't need to worry about pulling wires (but I do get to fab something from the ground up for the project car, wish me luck!)<P>So... any tips on pulling the dash? (besides, remove lots of stuff, and pull?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  this will be my first dash-pull.<P>Thanks, I love pennock's!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000005||Wolfhound||10-01-2001||07:39 AM||||The dash isn't too difficult. Two bolts under the dash speakers, two under the lower corners. You have to remove the insturment cluster, the shift surround, Radio heater control facia and housing, Aux. gauges if it has them. lower the steering wheel and it will come out.<BR>I think that's everything!<BR>Coy||||reg||1
Z||000006||wantav8||10-01-2001||03:42 PM||||I have the same problem, I have everything unbolted and can only move the ducting part out about an inch before it gets really tight. Do I need to pull harder?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Jim Jimenez||10-02-2001||01:48 AM||||You fellows are having trouble getting the heater/AC module out (which comes out in one piece) because the lower heater deflector inside the car ia still attached. Also, you need to remove the brake booster for clearence. <BR>  If you haven't done this before, study the GM service manual. The ducts in the instrument cluster and for the defroster are the same between AC and non AC cars, but they, too have to be removed before you can get the heater/AC module out. <BR>||||reg||1
